WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.880
If you had to choose between opening up a store&nbsp;
where you worked at the scheduled time or going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:08.720
to a client’s house to secure a $15,000&nbsp;
deal for your boss, which would you choose?

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:14.280
When he was 23, Mark Cuban faced that very&nbsp;
decision. He chose the deal over the store.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.720
While the choice seemed right to him,&nbsp;
it got him fired. More about that later.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.720
To understand the man behind such bold&nbsp;
decisions, we have to go back to when Mark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.920
Cuban was just 12 years old and sold garbage&nbsp;
bags door to door in a suburb of Pittsburgh.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:31.320
He later sold powdered milk,&nbsp;
which wasn’t much of a step up.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.120
He recalled in his book, “How&nbsp;
to Win at the Sport of Business”

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:38.560
“In every job, I would justify it in my&nbsp;
mind, whether I loved it or hated it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.920
that I was getting paid to learn&nbsp;
and every experience would be of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.960
value when I figured out what&nbsp;
I wanted to do when I grew up.”

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.120
He figured out very quickly that&nbsp;
he wanted to run his own business.&nbsp;

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.600
And boy has he been successful.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.560
From co-founding Broadcast.com,&nbsp;
a pioneer in online broadcasting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.200
to owning the Dallas Mavericks,&nbsp;
to his iconic role on Shark Tank.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.680
If I offered you $30 million for&nbsp;
the company, would you take it?

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.040
Mark Cuban is worth $5.2 billion.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.960
Yet even Cuban grappled with self-doubt&nbsp;
when he started out, admitting:

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.600
“In reality, I had as much doubt&nbsp;
as confidence. I was just hoping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.560
the confidence would win over the doubt and&nbsp;
it would all work out for the best.” page 3

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.920
After graduating from Indiana University in the&nbsp;
early eighties, he looked for a job where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:25.840
could learn more about computers. The personal&nbsp;
computing industry was starting to take off.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.120
He took a job at Mellon Bank in his&nbsp;
hometown of Pittsburgh where he took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.480
old manual systems at small banks and&nbsp;
converted them to automated systems.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.120
He didn’t last long there, all of nine months. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:40.400
next stint trying to franchise the TV&nbsp;
repair business lasted eight months.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.840
Then, at the age of 23, he&nbsp;
took off to Dallas, Texas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.680
where, in his words, he pursued&nbsp;
“fun, sun, money and women”.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.560
He crashed at a friend’s apartment, sleeping&nbsp;
on the couch or the floor, lived off bar food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.760
and bartended at night while hunting for&nbsp;
a job in the computer industry by day.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:02.000
He managed to convince a store to hire him to&nbsp;
sell software despite his lack of experience.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.320
When the interviewers asked: “What do you do if a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.320
customer has a question about a software&nbsp;
package and you don’t know the answer?”

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.600
He responded, “I would look it up in the&nbsp;
manual and find the answer for them.”

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.560
Not only was that a good answer,&nbsp;
but that’s actually what he did.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.080
He’d spend every night reading computer manuals to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.200
learn everything he could about&nbsp;
the products he was selling.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.640
He felt this would give him an “edge” over others.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:28.600
And he believes his passion for learning is&nbsp;
one of the main reasons he’s so successful.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.280
Clearly, it worked because&nbsp;
customers were so impressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.240
with his knowledge that they hired him&nbsp;
to install software at their offices.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:40.120
He charged $25 an hour for consulting,&nbsp;
split between him and the store - and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.920
extra cash allowed him to move into&nbsp;
an apartment with his own bedroom.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.040
Cuban realized he loved working with technology.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.960
But…he hated having a boss.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.000
Nine months into his new job, a&nbsp;
client asked him to come into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.520
office to close a $15,000 deal. The&nbsp;
problem was the client wanted him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:00.920
come at the same time he was expected&nbsp;
to open up the software store at 9 am

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.360
He chose to close the sale&nbsp;
rather than open up shop.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:08.440
Cuban recalled: “We all have to make that&nbsp;
“make or break” call to follow orders or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:12.800
do what you know is right. I followed&nbsp;
my first instinct: Close the sale.”

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.640
He got a coworker to open up the shop instead,&nbsp;
however, his boss wasn’t impressed and fired him.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:23.680
While most people would have been crushed by&nbsp;
the firing, Cuban had a different perspective.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:24.360
He thought:

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:29.080
“...it never really matters how many times&nbsp;
you fail. You only have to be right once.”

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:34.160
Getting fired was a blessing in disguise - it&nbsp;
ignited his drive to start his own company.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.920
He created MicroSolutions, Inc.,&nbsp;
which sold personal computers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.480
software and helped companies&nbsp;
and individuals install them.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:43.960
He called up all the clients&nbsp;
he had worked with at the shop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:47.160
informing them he was now on his&nbsp;
own, and sought their business.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:51.640
They were hesitant to hire him since he&nbsp;
didn’t have employees or even a company phone.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:55.160
So, Cuban's initial approach&nbsp;
focused on building trust.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:58.920
When he tried to convince one company to buy&nbsp;
an accounting software he was selling, he said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:03.840
he’d install it for free, and offered to refund&nbsp;
100% of their money if the software didn't work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:09.080
for them. They’d just need to pay the $500 it took&nbsp;
for Cuban to buy the software from the publisher.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:12.360
And in exchange, he could use them as a reference.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:16.920
Instead, he was investing in&nbsp;
building a client base and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:21.335
reputation, which put him in a better&nbsp;
position to negotiate future deals.

00:04:21.335 --> 00:04:21.395
First company, Architectural&nbsp;
Lighting (Cuban thinks he sold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.395 --> 00:04:21.456
them on Peachtree Accounting)&nbsp;
- they were looking for a time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.456 --> 00:04:21.520
and billing accounting system to&nbsp;
track their work with clients)

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.480
He got along so well with another&nbsp;
client that its CEO, Martin Woodall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.920
ended up becoming a partner in&nbsp;
his business, MicroSolutions.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:30.800
They were the perfect match.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.480
Martin was detail-oriented and&nbsp;
“anal” as Cuban described him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:38.320
whereas Cuban was disorganized&nbsp;
and not a details person.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.160
Cuban understood that success in business isn't&nbsp;
just about individual talent but also about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:47.480
surrounding yourself with the right people&nbsp;
who fill in the gaps in your own abilities.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:50.600
Or in his words, “Don’t lie to yourself. Each of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:55.320
us has to face the reality of who&nbsp;
we are and what we are.” Page 40

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.680
They even weathered a major financial scam when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.320
their secretary stole nearly&nbsp;
all of the company’s money!

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.160
Cuban says she whited out the names of the&nbsp;
checks he wrote to vendors and altered them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:11.800
to her own name, redirecting $83,000&nbsp;
of the company’s $85,000 to herself.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.680
While most people would have&nbsp;
FREAKED out with a capital F,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:17.600
he didn’t because he thought it a waste of energy.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.920
And the hard lesson he learned was that “No&nbsp;
one was going to cover my obligations but me.”

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:26.800
He returned to work, put his head&nbsp;
down, and searched for new ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.560
reading every book related to&nbsp;
computers that he could get his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:33.960
hands on - learning about the features that&nbsp;
worked well and the bugs in the software.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:37.000
He still reads for three hours every day.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:40.200
After seven years, he sold MicroSolutions in 1990&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:44.360
to CompuServe, a subsidiary&nbsp;
of H&amp;R Block for $6 million.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:48.280
He took the money and started trading tech&nbsp;
stocks at a time when they were soaring.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:52.040
It was during this time that he&nbsp;
identified a gap in the digital world

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:57.600
While hanging out with his college buddy Todd&nbsp;
Wagner, they whined about the fact that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:02.480
couldn’t listen to the basketball games from&nbsp;
Indiana University while living in Texas.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:04.600
So, they decided to launch the audio streaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:07.880
service AudioNet with just $8,000.

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:11.160
Initially, AudioNet focused on&nbsp;
broadcasting college sports.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:14.880
Then it went from audio to video -&nbsp;
and this was a decade before YouTube.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.280
AudioNet eventually became Broadcast.com.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.600
They recognized the potential of the&nbsp;
platform beyond sports to broadcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.880
anything over the internet, including&nbsp;
shareholder meetings and earnings calls.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:29.560
The idea was to reach people during&nbsp;
the day when they were in the office.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:36.880
Yahoo! ended up purchasing Broadcast.com in&nbsp;
1999 for a whopping $5.7 billion in stock.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:41.400
However, Yahoo! would shut down&nbsp;
much of its broadcast services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.520
and Broadcast.com was eventually discontinued.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:49.200
In hindsight, it’s been called one of the&nbsp;
worst internet acquisitions of all time.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:53.760
For Mark Cuban, it catapulted&nbsp;
him into the billionaire’s club.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:56.680
The first thing he did? Was buying a plane.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:02.040
The asset he values most is time - and his&nbsp;
Gulfstream V business jet bought him more of it.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:05.040
He didn’t even see it in person&nbsp;
when he bought it online for $40&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:10.280
million - setting a Guinness World Record&nbsp;
for the largest online purchase at the time.

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:12.280
The Gulfstream was just the beginning.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:15.760
The iconic purchase he’s&nbsp;
most known for was in 2000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:21.000
when the diehard basketball made bought&nbsp;
the Dallas Mavericks for $285 million.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:25.600
What’s that team worth now?&nbsp;
$2.5 billion. Goddamn! EBITDA.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:28.640
Cuban brought a fresh approach&nbsp;
to NBA team ownership.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:33.120
He ditched the box seats billionaire owners&nbsp;
were accustomed to and sat behind the bench,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:35.200
notoriously yelling at the refs.

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:37.040
This hands-on approach also changed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:42.080
team’s atmosphere and the Mavericks won&nbsp;
their first NBA championship in 2011.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:45.480
For Cuban, business is more&nbsp;
intense than any sport.&nbsp;

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:48.520
With sports, you know how long&nbsp;
the game will last, usually.

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:54.840
In business, competition is 24 hours&nbsp;
a day, 7 days a week, 365 days a year.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:58.160
He once quipped, “If your girlfriend&nbsp;
asks you which is more important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:01.080
them or your business, you give the right answer.”

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:05.080
He’s so fired up about competition&nbsp;
that after becoming filthy rich,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:06.680
he didn’t rest on his laurels.

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:10.160
Cuban launched the high-definition&nbsp;
television network HDNet.

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:13.840
He co-founded the entertainment company 2929&nbsp;
Entertainment, which develops and produces films.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:18.480
And in 2022, he launched a drug company&nbsp;
that promises steep discounts on hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:22.880
of prescription drugs to tackle&nbsp;
the high price of drugs in the U.S.

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:25.360
These days, instead of 24 hours a day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:31.040
7 days a week, 365 days a year, he’s&nbsp;
cut back…to a mere 18 hours a day.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:33.280
To spend precious time with his family.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:37.040
He met his wife Tiffany at&nbsp;
a gym in Dallas in 1997.

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:39.920
They have three children and&nbsp;
live in a chateau-style home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:42.720
larger than five basketball courts.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:47.800
When he was young, he’d drive by big houses like&nbsp;
his every weekend and wonder who lived there.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:49.160
What did they do for a living?

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:51.920
How did they manage to afford a place like that?

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:57.440
The next time you pass by a sprawling estate like&nbsp;
Mark Cuban used to, remember that countless hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:02.720
failures, blood, sweat, and tears&nbsp;
likely paved the way to that doorstep.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.600
Self-education was pivotal in Cuban’s journey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:09.600
proving that with the right resources,&nbsp;
anyone can master complex subjects.

